1. **GAP — the supplied record does not establish the claimed 2/2 acceptance.**

> “**THIS NOTE IS ACCEPTED at 2/2** — PE9 … CLEAN … and PE10 … CLEAN”

But the supplied PE9 report says:

> “This is the arc’s **FIRST clean pass** … the count starts at **1/2**”

PE8 was explicitly not clean, and no PE10 report is pasted. Thus the second clean pass—its target blob, independence, findings, and claimed 326,206/0 and 106,562/0 legs—cannot be audited. On the provided record, only 1/2 is certified.

2. **GAP — the shear is not lattice-preserving, so JB-VTX(c)’s lattice-data claim does not follow.**

> “a side of print slope −h/e in lowest terms has its **lattice data preserved**: degree d = (abscissa run)/e is the same integer in both frames”

Yet

> “Σ_q : (i,u) ↦ (i,e_q·u+h_q·i)”

has determinant \(e_q\), not \(\pm1\). For \(e_q=2,h_q=1\), the integral edge from \((0,1)\) to \((2,0)\), slope \(-1/2\), maps to the horizontal edge from \((0,2)\) to \((2,2)\). The original edge has primitive horizontal step \(2\); the image has ambient-lattice step \(1\) and contains the additional lattice point \((1,2)\). Abscissa run is preserved, but lattice length/data is not.

Calling \((\text{run})/e\) the “harness grid count” requires the separately asserted attained-grid structure in JB-VTX(d4). That subclaim carries the membership/u≥0 residue and has no machine leg, while matrix row 10 gives JB-VTX(c) neither condition. The conditionality matrix therefore under-fences this part of clause (c).

3. **GAP — W2-C3’s measured-above region is still used by statements labelled PROVED.**

The body uses:

> “W2-C3 chamber off the byte perimeter”

and claims:

> “JB-TREE … **PROVED** (attempt grade)”

But E-2 says W2-C3 is only:

> “proved-to-first-twist (38/44) / **measured above (6/44, riding W2-OPEN-2)**”

Acceptance of the supplier note does not convert its explicitly measured-above box into a proof. Counter-configuration: any of those six above-first-twist transported-chamber instances. The machine observations may hold, but the admissible-representative premise used to identify the tree with the classifier’s division is not proved there. The annex records the re-grade but does not propagate “measured above” into the JB-TREE grade surfaces.

4. **GAP — byte inequality does not justify the repeated claim that the semantic identities are false off-perimeter.**

> “Off that perimeter … (VAL) is then **FALSE, not merely unproved**”

and:

> “off it … clause (a) is **FALSE, not merely unproved**”

Different representative bytes do not imply different induced valuations on the relevant coefficients. At the simplest pointwise counter-configuration, take \(f=1\): its sole digit is \(A_0=1\), and both (VAL) and the shear identity hold independently of all key bytes. More generally, distinct equivalent representatives can agree semantically on lower-degree inputs. If “FALSE” means failure somewhere in the off-perimeter stratum, the pasted note supplies no witness or derivation; if it means failure for every off-perimeter instance, \(f=1\) refutes it.

